-- Pass 100 §9 THE CONVERSION ALGEBRA — a relation with a declaration face, an
-- enumerable trie, and one composition law. gap[082].
--
--     to(micron)(inch) = stretch@lossless
--     for src, conv in to[micron]
--     inch:from(foot)(3.0)          -- an edge NOBODY DECLARED
--
-- WHAT THIS FIXTURE EXISTS TO CATCH. `to` was not a relation. Four sites in
-- `src/codegen.zig` compared the literal string "to" and lowered the call
-- directly, so the conversion universe was exactly the primitives the code
-- generator had been taught by hand: 1647 call sites, six destinations, and
-- ZERO declared edges. The declaration face §9 is written in terms of
-- (`to(str)(u32) = digits`) parsed, passed `duo check`, and then died in the C
-- backend on "expression is not assignable" — it lowered to
-- `lua_to_str(tostring("u32")) = digits`. The enumeration face died on "use of
-- undeclared identifier 'to'". Neither was a missing feature exactly; both were
-- an ABSENT MECHANISM wearing a string comparison.
--
-- NO NEW GRAMMAR (A2 NNS-FIRST). `to(dest)(src) = conv` is an ordinary
-- assignment statement and `conv@class` is the postfix anchor that already
-- parses to a field walk. Nothing here is a new token, sigil or keyword; the
-- forms existed and had no meaning.
--
-- SIX AUTHORED EDGES, SIX DERIVED. `micron` is the canonical hub; `inch`,
-- `milli` and `foot` each declare their two edges to and from it. The three
-- spoke-to-spoke pairs in both directions — inch<->milli, inch<->foot,
-- milli<->foot — are NEVER DECLARED and are answered by composition through
-- the hub. SER = 12/6 = 2.00 at N = 4, and both numbers matter: the ratio is a
-- function of ecosystem size, so a SER without its N is not a measurement.
--
-- WHY EVERY EDGE IS f64 AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT. The same six edges over i64
-- would make each division NARROWING (25401 micron is not a whole inch), and
-- the class lattice would then REFUSE all six derivations rather than silently
-- compose into a lossy conversion. That refusal is the companion fixture
-- `relationclass.duo`. An algebra that composes anything is not safer than no
-- algebra — it is more dangerous, because adding a descriptor could quietly
-- change an existing program's answer.
--
-- VERIFIED BY VALUE, and the value is chosen to kill the degenerate answers.
-- A composition that resolves and returns 0 — this tree's known failure mode,
-- the one `tonumber("abc")` already has — prints 153, 62 or 179 depending on
-- which hop collapsed. An enumeration that yields nothing prints 173. A trie
-- that answered the pair conversions from a transposed key would not compile at
-- all. Only real composition over a real trie prints 189.
-- expect: 189

stretch = (v: f64): f64
    v * 25400.0

shrink = (v: f64): f64
    v / 25400.0

swell = (v: f64): f64
    v * 1000.0

shrivel = (v: f64): f64
    v / 1000.0

expand = (v: f64): f64
    v * 304800.0

compress = (v: f64): f64
    v / 304800.0

to(micron)(inch) = stretch@lossless
to(inch)(micron) = shrink@lossless
to(micron)(milli) = swell@lossless
to(milli)(micron) = shrivel@lossless
to(micron)(foot) = expand@lossless
to(foot)(micron) = compress@lossless

main = (): i64
    -- Three conversions, none of them declared. Each one is the hub composition
    -- and each one is exact in f64, so the answer is an integer or the
    -- derivation is wrong.
    feet: f64 = inch:from(foot)(3.0)
    bore: f64 = milli:from(inch)(5.0)
    span: f64 = inch:from(milli)(254.0)

    n = 0
    n = n + feet:to(i64)
    n = n + bore:to(i64)
    n = n + span:to(i64)

    -- The trie is enumerable, and the loop is over the SOURCES that reach the
    -- hub. `src` carries the descriptor name as the string fact it is, which is
    -- what the name-length sum proves — a loop that ran the right number of
    -- times over empty names would print 176.
    for src, conv in to[micron]
        n = n + 1
        n = n + #src

    print("{n}")
    0
